<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Marciano calls and is seen the same day for six days of a burning, gnawing pain in the upper middle of his stomach, worst at night and before meals and eased a little by food and milk. He has been taking naproxen 500 mg once or twice nearly every day since intake, after physical therapy and swimming, and sometimes on an empty stomach in the morning. He has not vomited and has had no black or bloody stools, no trouble swallowing, no weight loss beyond what he intended, and no fever. He drinks little alcohol and does not smoke. He has no chest pain, and the pain does not come on with exertion. His back pain is now mild. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Six days ago, after about seven weeks of near-daily naproxen</td></tr><tr><td>Location</td><td>Upper middle abdomen, not radiating</td></tr><tr><td>Quality</td><td>Burning and gnawing</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>4 out of 10, waking him once a night</td></tr><tr><td>Timing</td><td>Worst at night and before meals</td></tr><tr><td>Modifying factors</td><td>Eased by food and milk; worse after a naproxen dose on an empty stomach</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>No vomiting, black stools, blood, dysphagia, weight loss or fever</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

